just a word of introduction for someone  who needs no introduction Sharon Coogler  gales university chaplain is our speaker  today I just wanted to tell you a little  bit about my observations of Sharon in  the last week or so when i started in  chaplaincy my mentor father Jack Kelly  said that chaplains at universities work  for five days a year and the rest is fun  but those five days are the worst five  days of your life and Sharon this last  two weeks has done incredible work that  she can't talk about and I can't talk  about behind the scenes to help with any  lays family after her murder and then  with Sylvia Bingham's death this week  Sylvia Bingham 09 she's done a lot with  incredible kindness love but also  strength the strength that you need to  get through these things and it's a  privilege to have her here Yale for me  to work with her and for us to have her  here at the university church Sharon  thank you for those kind words Ian the  whole chaplaincy staff has been truly  heaven-sent this week just two weeks ago  this sermon was going to be about  something else two weeks ago I was  imagining a beautiful late-september day  and our almost childlike wonder at  finding ourselves together worshiping in  this tent why are we in a tent who is in  Patel how did this come about what does  this mean these were the questions I was  looking forward to pondering with you  these were the questions that were  inspiring my reflections on the text of  the day and yet now why who how what  those same interrogatives are still  there with us yet in a very different  context there is a kind of fragility in  the air right now that defies  description we have all been touched by  deep deep sorrow each of us in our own  way some of us are fearful and cannot  wrap our minds much less our spirits  around the events of the last several  days Annie lays death has shaken us to  our core and reminded us of an  all-too-common darkness that we sell  them have to directly encounter much  less touch here in what some have called  the Yale bubble and yet somewhere every  minute in our world unspeakable  senseless violence happens we find  ourselves asking the questions of the  ages about good and evil chance and  Providence and how they manifest  themselves in our world we are not  asking them in the abstract now these  questions are very real to us and they  have a human face I know my dear sisters  and brothers that I cannot make sense of  what happened to Annie for you much less  for myself nor can i or  will I tie it up in a neat theological  bow for you or me to swallow and then  move on I can only speak to our human  response to unbearable heartbreak as a  chaplain I view it is nothing short of a  sacred privilege to be with someone in a  time of sorrow and loss the time passes  in a series of moments moments which are  heartbreaking overwhelming leaving me  feeling helpless to ease the pain yet  the moments are most certainly holy holy  may seem like an odd description for  such a thing but it is what I know to be  true these moments open us to each other  and to what is really real in ways that  can't often be explained and maybe  shouldn't be these moments are not lived  in our heads they are experienced in our  breathing in and in our breathing out  although there were difficult  distractions from the media and rampant  rumor there was holiness the fragility  in the air lent itself to perhaps a  deeper kind of mindfulness and how we  are present with one another we held  hands tighter we hugged longer we  breathe in and out with purpose and yes  even gratitude Annie's family and all  who knew her are left to pick up pick up  the pieces of their future born out of  this tragedy but we are here to help  this is what the human family does and  has done for as long as our stories have  unfolded we stand with each other arms  firmly holding each other up we comfort  each other and we get on down on our  knees if one of us has fallen this  morning in our second lesson James asks  us who is wise and understanding among  you show by your good life that your  works are done with gentleness born of  wisdom  he cautions us not to live in our heads  that wisdom is understood in our  behavior again James but the wisdom from  above is first pure than peaceable  gentle willing to yield full of mercy  and good fruits without a trace of  partiality or hypocrisy James does not  skirt the issue of evil he faces it head  heads on and he points to its potential  in each one of us the great  spiritualists and sage to Martin Luther  King jr. the Reverend Howard Thurman  gives us an eloquent description of what  it means to foster wisdom from above as  one walks through the twist in terms of  life he writes I seek a gracious spirit  in dealing with the in justices of the  world this I do not confuse with  softness or fear or cowardness or  sentimentality I must know clearly the  evilness of evil and recognize it for  what it is stark brutal terrifying I  must oppose it and place full weight of  whatever forces I have thoroughly  against it at the same time I cannot  exceed escape the fact that every  judgment is a self judgment that even as  I resist evil I share the guilt of evil  it is this sense of sharing the guilt of  evil that should inspire the gracious  spirit in dealing with injustice James  beckons us draw near to God draw near to  God those are the words that fueled my  soul these last several days as God's  own presence was what held so many  during our collective shock when tragedy  strikes we stand at the crossroads  between hope and despair torn and  hurting despair cements us and the  present hope on the other hand sends us  to look around dark corners trusting in  a tomb  morrow we cannot see a tomorrow that may  at this moment seemed terribly  precarious and uncertain long ago i  learned that hope and despair not  opposites they are cut from the same  cloth made from the same material shaped  from the very same circumstances most of  all every life finds itself forced to  choose one from another one day at a  time one circumstance after another the  only difference between the two is that  despair shapes an attitude of the mind  whereas hope creates a quality of a soul  despair colors the way we look at things  makes us fearful and suspicious of the  future it coaxes us into a feeling of  powerlessness with the present the death  of an eel a signaled a sudden ending and  rendered each of us stunned and sour  full we as a family as a community felt  profound to sprayer we froze in our  tracks what could such senseless  violence mean how could it happen here  how could it happen within the fold we  were forced to ask ourselves where is  the light where is the hope in all our  intellectual sophistication we are often  without words dumbfounded in the face of  life and death we frantically search for  ways to dull the pain and this can take  on different forms we might lash out we  might retreat we must seek to be wise  instead of simply smart we must save her  a continual hunger for something more we  must allow ourselves to be restored it  is essential that we continue to long  for experiences of joy and comfort to  listen for the healing voice of God in  the silence of our souls this is what  will make us new again many of us saw on  monday night at annies vigil that we  can't get there by walking through the  pain alone we must get there as a  community yes we felt completely  off-balance and yes the last few days  words and reason have failed us but we  have never been abandoned God's abiding  love has been and continues to be  fertile in the darkness we have shed  tears and we need to be held close by  one another there is profound sorrow but  there is also human connection and  through that connection there is hope  the late theologian and poet henri  nouwen wrote hope means to keep living  amid desperation and to keep humming in  the darkness hope is knowing there is  love it is a trust in tomorrow it is  falling asleep and waking again when the  Sun rises in the midst of a gay lat see  it is to discover land in the eyes of  another it is to see that you are  understood as long as there is still  hope there will also be prayer and you  will be held in God's own hands my  sisters and brothers together we are  back into a new beginning a beginning  that for many of us may feel beyond our  strength we are summoned to hope again  to want tomorrow to celebrate the lives  of an eel a and Sylvia Bingham in our  tomorrows hope takes life in its own  terms it knows that whatever happens God  lives in it and hope expects love will  conquer evil and that always always we  are never alone despair says there is no  where to go but here hope says that our  tender loving God is waiting for us  somewhere else the impassioned prayer of  Howard Thurman was to ask God to match  our darts of despair with the treasures  of our dreams we need to take each  other's hand and begin let us begin  again to hope let us dream let us draw  near to God finally I'm so very grateful  that this sunday is a Eucharist sunday  for we need to be around this table  desperately right now as we draw near to  God around the table in this tent let me  point to mark's gospel which is such a  beautiful illustration illustration of  servant leadership or social status even  religious affiliation is far from the  point we are in this tent this morning  because our Jewish brothers and sisters  needed the considerable space that Patel  chapel offers in order to celebrate Rosh  Hashanah our Muslim community who  normally uses Patel in the evening  during Ramadan to break their fast we're  over at st. Thomas More last night for  the same reason we as richly diverse  religious communities are demonstrating  holy hospitality in numerous settings  and though it has been chaotic to  conceptualize and even to orchestrate  this morning it somehow feels just right  walls do not make a church you and I  make church through selfless acts of  love and welcome it has been written  that radical servant leadership is not  just for the church it is also the  witness of the church to the world you  my beloved sisters and brothers of  university church are a beautiful  embodiment of this witness today you  welcome the stranger you make room for  the other and you are walking humbly  with your God amen
 

